---

# Router Simplification

Instead of:

```text
auth.py
oauth.py
profile.py
session.py
```

we now have:

```text
oauth.py
profile.py
sessions.py
tokens.py
health.py
```

Because **OAuth is the authentication mechanism**.

---

# Service Simplification

Old:

```text
auth_service.py

oauth_service.py

profile_service.py

token_service.py
```

New:

```text
oauth_service.py

profile_service.py

session_service.py

token_service.py
```

No generic `auth_service`.

---

# Infrastructure Simplification

Remove

```text
password.py

hashing.py

bcrypt.py
```

Keep only

```text
jwt.py
```

because passwords no longer exist.

---

# Domain Simplification

Instead of

```text
User

Session

OAuthAccount

PasswordCredential

RefreshToken
```

use

```text
User

OAuthIdentity

Session

RefreshToken
```

`OAuthIdentity` stores information about the external provider.

---

# Database Tables

You can simplify the schema to something like:

```text
users
------------
id
email
name
picture_url
role
created_at
updated_at
```

```text
oauth_identities
----------------
id
user_id
provider          (google)
provider_user_id
email
access_token      (optional, if needed)
refresh_token     (optional)
expires_at
```

```text
sessions
------------
id
user_id
device
ip_address
user_agent
last_activity
expires_at
```

```text
refresh_tokens
----------------
id
session_id
token_hash
expires_at
revoked_at
```

---

# API Endpoints

Instead of dozens of authentication endpoints, the service becomes very compact:

```text
GET    /api/v1/oauth/google/login

GET    /api/v1/oauth/google/callback

POST   /api/v1/tokens/refresh

POST   /api/v1/sessions/logout

POST   /api/v1/sessions/logout-all

GET    /api/v1/profile

PATCH  /api/v1/profile

GET    /api/v1/sessions

DELETE /api/v1/sessions/{session_id}

GET    /api/v1/health
```

---

## I would also simplify the `shared` package

Since you've standardized on Google OAuth only, I'd remove password-related utilities from `shared` as well.

Instead of:

```text
shared/auth/
├── jwt.py
├── password.py
├── oauth.py
├── permissions.py
└── roles.py
```

I'd keep:

```text
shared/auth/
├── jwt.py
├── oauth.py
├── permissions.py
├── roles.py
└── claims.py
```

No password hashing, bcrypt, or credential utilities are needed. This keeps the shared package aligned with your actual authentication strategy and avoids carrying abstractions you'll never use.
